  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong upper level ridge of high pressure will sprawl out over\nthe Deep South this week. The persistent low clouds and drizzle\nleft behind from the departing remnants of Isaias are finally \nstarting to erode from the west, and this trend will continue \nthrough the remainder of the afternoon, with skies clearing \neventually.\n\nModel soundings suggest we will not fully decouple tonight as\nlow level southwesterly flow remains engaged. So, despite\nabundant low level moisture from the recent rains, the light\nbreeze should keep fog more confined to the deeper mountain \nvalleys.\n\nWhile some mid and high level clouds will be found from time to\ntime on Tuesday, enough sunshine is expected to boost highs \ninto the 70s and 80s, some 10 to 15 degrees warmer than normal.\nSimilar temperatures are expected on Wednesday as well. See\nClimate section below for details on how these temperatures\ncompare to records.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLonger range forecast models are coming into good agreement \nthat a front will drop south into our region by late in the day \non Wednesday. The front gets hung up over us through Thursday,\ndue to the ridge to our south. A strong cold front then pushes \nthrough on Friday. This pattern suggests the potential for \nseveral rounds of showers and thunderstorms with flooding and \neven a severe thunderstorm or two possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nConfidence is increasing that a decent shot of cold air funnels\ninto the region over the weekend behind the cold front.\nTemperatures will likely not get out of the 50s on Saturday with\nlows Saturday night falling into the 30s. This poses a risk for\nwidespread frost and maybe even a freeze in some locations.",
